# 1. Research Objective

Do NOT treat this project as a general validation of latent communication.

Existing work such as Interlat has already established that LLM agents can communicate through latent representations.

Existing hybrid communication work such as HyLaT has also established that text and latent channels can be combined.

Therefore, the main research problem is:

[
\boxed{
\text{How should humor-specific intermediate information be allocated between text and latent channels?}
}
]

More specifically:

> Which parts of humor planning should be explicitly verbalized, and which parts should remain in continuous latent representations before caption generation?

The target architecture is:

[
\text{Image}
\rightarrow
\text{Humor Planner}
\rightarrow
{
\text{explicit text anchors},
\text{continuous latent states}
}
\rightarrow
\text{Caption Generator}.
]

---

# 2. Main Scientific Hypothesis

The central hypothesis is:

> Explicit semantic commitments are well suited for textual communication, while divergent associative information is better preserved in latent space.

For multimodal humor generation, use the following reduced cognitive decomposition:

[
\boxed{
C
\rightarrow
A
\rightarrow
M
}
]

where:

### C — Conflict / Script Opposition

What expectation is violated?

What two semantic scripts conflict?

Example:

```text
normal corporate meeting
vs
dog behaving like the manager
```

---

### A — Association

What remote concepts are activated by that conflict?

Example:

```text
boss
obedience
training
promotion
corporate hierarchy
HR
workplace politics
dog commands
```

This stage may represent multiple weak and competing semantic directions simultaneously.

---

### M — Humor Mechanism / Resolution

How should the association become a joke?

Examples:

```text
role reversal
analogy
exaggeration
misunderstanding
irony
anthropomorphism
```

---

The primary hypothesis is:

[
\boxed{
C_{\text{text}}
+
A_{\text{latent}}
+
M_{\text{text}}
}
]

will be more effective than representing all three stages entirely through natural language.

This is a hypothesis, not an assumption.

The experiments must test it.

